; os, on Node — the LIBRARY layer, not the target's.
;
; TOTALISATION, AND WHY NOTHING NEW WAS NEEDED IN THE LANGUAGE.
;
; A host offers a PARTIAL function `f : A ⇀ B`; partiality is the whole content
; of "it can fail". Partial maps `A ⇀ B` are exactly total maps `A → B + 1`, and
; with information on the failure `A → B + E` — a coproduct, which sums.md has
; and which `(values b e)` already carries across a boundary. So the TYPE of a
; fallible call is host-independent and already in the language.
;
; What varies is the host's MECHANISM for saying which summand you got:
;
;   Go           a second return value          `B × E` plus a convention
;   JavaScript   throw                          a partial map and an escape
;   Java         throw, checked                 the same, with E in the types
;   windows      a sentinel and GetLastError    `B + 1`, niche-encoded
;
; NO HOST GIVES THE COPRODUCT. Go's `(T, error)` is a PRODUCT with a discipline
; and `err-nil` is the discriminator that totalises it, so this repository has
; been totalising since the day it could open a file and never called it that.
;
; It follows that a target declares two things — the CALL that produces the
; pair, and the DISCRIMINATOR that reads it — and the compiler learns nothing
; about exceptions. On this host the totalisation is a `try`/`catch`, written
; here, in data.
;
; AND ON A THROWING HOST THERE IS NO SMALLER HONEST BINDING. `fs.readFileSync`
; cannot be declared `((path string)) (array (int 0 255))` and be true: it
; throws, and a signature that does not say so is a lie a program cannot see
; through. So the totalising form IS the binding, which is why nothing simpler
; sits underneath this in `targets/js/`.

(provides js os
  ; THE TEMPLATE ASSIGNS ITS RESULTS rather than being them, and that is the
  ; second of the two multi-result shapes the format has (`%r0`, `%r1`). A host
  ; whose call IS the tuple is destructured; a host that signals failure out of
  ; band has to be GIVEN somewhere to put the two values, because a `try`/`catch`
  ; is a statement and not an expression yielding a pair.
  ;
  ; `%r` is `%r0` at arity one, so this is the existing result hole at the arity
  ; the call actually has.
  ;
  ; THE ELEMENT RANGE IS DECLARED — a file is bytes — which is what gives the
  ; reads their width and lets `(src i)` be used in arithmetic without a refusal
  ; (elemwidth-2026-08-27). `readFileSync` gives a Buffer, which is a
  ; Uint8Array, and indexes 0..255 exactly as the declaration says.
  (sig ReadFile ((path string)) (tuple (array (int 0 255)) error) (host expr
      "try { %r0 = fs.readFileSync(%s); %r1 = null; } catch (e) { %r0 = null; %r1 = e; }"
      (import "node:fs")))

  (sig WriteFile ((path string) (data (array (int 0 255))) (perm int)) error (host expr
      "try { fs.writeFileSync(%s, Buffer.from(%s), { mode: %s }); %r0 = null; } catch (e) { %r0 = e; }"
      (import "node:fs")))

  ; The discriminator. `null` because that is what the templates above put
  ; there — one file says both halves, so the two cannot disagree.
  (sig err-nil ((e error)) bool pure (host expr "%s === null"))

  ; Bytes to text, ONCE, at the boundary. `TextDecoder` is the platform's own
  ; UTF-8 decode and needs no import, and it IS d (ADR 0030): the WHATWG decoder
  ; is the Unicode Standard's maximal-subpart substitution, which Go and Java
  ; implement to match.
  (sig text-of ((b (array (int 0 255)))) string pure (host expr
      "new TextDecoder().decode(Uint8Array.from(%s))"))

  ; `process.argv` is [node, script, …] where Go's `os.Args` is [prog, …], so
  ; the first is dropped and a program sees the same shape on both hosts. That
  ; is the parasite model doing its job: the difference is stated once, in the
  ; cell that knows about it, and no program learns the hosts disagree.
  (sig Args () (array string) (host expr "process.argv.slice(1)"))
  (sig Getenv ((k string)) string pure (host expr "(process.env[%s] ?? \"\")")))
